---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Why Is My Battery Swollen? The Lithium Swelling Warning';
const description = 'A swollen lithium battery is gas build-up from electrolyte decomposition — overcharge, heat, age or a defect. Why it swells, why it is dangerous, and what to do.';
const canonical = '/lithium/swollen-battery/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Swollen lithium battery',
  articleSection: 'Lithium Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-16',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Why do lithium batteries swell?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Gas builds up inside when the electrolyte decomposes — driven by overcharge, overheating, deep over-discharge or aging. The pouch or case bulges as the pressure rises. Swelling is a failure sign, not a minor blemish.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Is a swollen battery dangerous?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes. A swollen cell is a failed cell with internal pressure — it can vent or ignite if punctured, crushed or further charged. Stop using and charging it, store it away from flammables, and recycle it through proper battery disposal.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lithium Knowledge · Problems · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Why Is My Battery Swollen? The Lithium Swelling Warning</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A battery that bulges is not a cosmetic issue — it is a chemical message from inside the cell, and the
        message is that the cell has failed.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Swelling is <strong>gas build-up</strong> from the electrolyte decomposing —
        driven by <strong>overcharge, overheating, deep over-discharge or aging</strong>. The case bulges as
        internal pressure rises. A swollen cell is a <strong>failed cell</strong>: stop using and charging it,
        keep it away from flammables, and recycle it through proper battery disposal.
      </div>

      <h2>Cause → Mechanism</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>How it produces gas</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Overcharge</strong></td><td>Excess voltage decomposes the electrolyte directly</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Heat</strong></td><td>High temperature accelerates every decomposition reaction</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Over-discharge</strong></td><td>Copper dissolution destabilises the chemistry on recharge</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Aging / defects</strong></td><td>SEI damage and internal shorts gas the cell slowly</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why it is a safety event</h2>
      <p>
        The gas is flammable, and the pressure distorts the layers inside — two steps toward
        <a href="/lithium/thermal-runaway/">thermal runaway</a> if the cell is punctured, crushed or charged again.
        This is why a swollen device battery should never be "checked" by pressing it, and why proper disposal —
        not the bin — is the end of its life (see <a href="/lithium/storage/">storage rules</a> for prevention).
      </p>

      <h2>What to do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Stop use and charging</strong> immediately — remove from the device where possible.</li>
        <li><strong>Store safely</strong> — cool, dry, away from flammables, not in a pocket or drawer.</li>
        <li><strong>Recycle properly</strong> — through battery collection points, never household waste.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My position:</strong> Swelling is the battery's own warning light — the only visible symptom of an
          invisible chemical failure — and the correct response is retirement, not investigation, because the
          curiosity that punctures a swollen cell is how small problems become fires.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>What drives this:</strong> The gas is flammable and the pressure is real; every additional charge or poke
          adds risk with zero benefit. Reading swelling as the failure signal it is — stop, store safe, recycle —
          is the whole protocol, and it is the cheapest safety discipline in lithium.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is my analysis, not a verified fact or purchasing guarantee. Swollen batteries are hazardous; dispose through proper channels.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Why do lithium batteries swell?</h3>
      <p>Gas builds up inside when the electrolyte decomposes — driven by overcharge, overheating, deep over-discharge or aging. The pouch or case bulges as the pressure rises. Swelling is a failure sign, not a minor blemish.</p>
      <h3>Is a swollen battery dangerous?</h3>
      <p>Yes. A swollen cell is a failed cell with internal pressure — it can vent or ignite if punctured, crushed or further charged. Stop using and charging it, store it away from flammables, and recycle it through proper battery disposal.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from cell-manufacturer safety guidance and battery engineering references.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Cell-manufacturer safety documentation — swelling and gassing failure modes.</li>
        <li>Battery engineering references — electrolyte decomposition and cell failure.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
